{"id":"1e4d533e-26ad-4263-91fc-d2c6388bc91f","object":"chat.completion","created":1785005921,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes_data = case[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n    required_symbols = set(case[\\\"required_symbols\\\"])\\n    node_dict = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    for n in nodes_data:\\n        nid = n[\\\"id\\\"]\\n        node_dict[nid] = n\\n        children[nid] = []\\n    for n in nodes_data:\\n        pid = n[\\\"parent\\\"]\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children[pid].append(n[\\\"id\\\"])\\n    all_symbols = {n[\\\"symbol\\\"] for n in node_dict.values()}\\n    if not required_symbols.issubset(all_symbols):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Required symbols not all present\\\")\\n    root_id = None\\n    for nid, n in node_dict.items():\\n        if n[\\\"parent\\\"] is None:\\n            root_id = nid\\n            break\\n    if root_id is None:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"No root node\\\")\\n    retained = set(node_dict.keys())\\n    candidates = [nid for nid, n in node_dict.items() if n[\\\"removable\\\"] and nid != root_id]\\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (-node_dict[x][\\\"depth\\\"], x))\\n    removed_order = []\\n    for nid in candidates:\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        subtree = set()\\n        stack = [nid]\\n        while stack:\\n            cur = stack.pop()\\n            if cur in subtree:\\n                continue\\n            subtree.add(cur)\\n            for child in children[cur]:\\n                stack.append(child)\\n        all_removable = all(node_dict[n][\\\"removable\\\"] for n in subtree)\\n        if not all_removable:\\n            continue\\n        remaining_symbols = set()\\n        for n_ret in retained:\\n            if n_ret not in subtree:\\n                remaining_symbols.add(node_dict[n_ret][\\\"symbol\\\"])\\n        if required_symbols.issubset(remaining_symbols):\\n            removed_order.append(nid)\\n            retained -= subtree\\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed_order, \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_ids}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write Python code for `solve(case)` that implements a grammar-guided node reduction algorithm based on the problem description. The task is to reduce a parse tree by removing removable nodes while preserving required symbols and some \"interestingness\" (though we don't have an exact oracle, only the requirement that all required symbols remain represented). The description mentions \"grammar_valid\" and \"interestingness_preserved\" but the actual condition seems to be: we can only remove a node if it is removable, not root, its symbol is not required, and after removal all required symbols remain present in the tree. Also we must consider deeper nodes first, and use node ID as tie-breaker.\n\nGiven the payload structure: each node has id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. The tree is built from these nodes. There is a list of required_symbols. We need to produce two lists: removed_node_ids (in order of removal) and retained_node_ids (sorted). The description says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids should be sorted (presumably alphabetically/numerically), and removed_node_ids should be in order of removal.\n\nAlgorithm: We start with all nodes. We need to decide which removable nodes to remove. The rule: we should try to remove nodes that are deeper first (higher depth). Among same depth, use node ID as tie-breaker (stable). We can remove a node only if it is removable, it is not the root (parent null), its symbol is not in required_symbols, and after removing it, all required symbols are still present in the tree (i.e., the required symbols must be represented by some node that remains). Also we must never remove a node that has retained children? Actually the description says: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That suggests that if a node has children that are retained, we cannot remove the parent? But wait, the typical algorithm: you remove a node and its children get reattached to its parent? Or you delete the whole subtree? The description says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" - but in Perses, you delete a node and its children are removed as well? Actually in parse tree reduction, you can delete a node and its entire subtree. But the requirement \"node with retained children\" might mean you cannot remove a node if it has children that you intend to keep? However, the typical Perses algorithm: you try to delete a node, and if interestingness fails, you keep it. But here we are to find a set of nodes to remove while preserving required symbols. Maybe we are to remove nodes one by one, and after removal, the children of removed node are also gone (since they were dependent on that non-terminal). The constraint \"never remove a node with retained children\" suggests that if a node has children that are still present (not removed), you cannot remove it because those children would become orphans? But in a tree, if you remove a node, its children are also removed because they are part of its subtree. So removal of a node implicitly removes its descendants. So we should not remove a node if we need to keep any of its descendants (because those descendants contain required symbols, or we want to keep them for some reason). However, the description says \"or a node with retained children\" - meaning if a node has children that are retained (i.e., not removed), then that node cannot be removed because removing it would also remove its children. So we must only remove leaf nodes or nodes whose entire subtree we decide to remove. But the algorithm might involve removing nodes in some order, and after removal, the tree changes. We need to simulate.\n\nGiven the complexity and the fact that this is a programming puzzle, we likely need to implement a greedy removal: iterate over nodes sorted by depth descending, and for each that is removable and not root and not required symbol, check if removing it (and its entire subtree) would still leave all required symbols present in the remaining tree. If yes, remove it. But \"node with retained children\" means we cannot remove a node if any of its children are still in the tree (i.e., not removed yet). Since we process deeper first, we will remove leaves first, then their parents become leaves, etc. So that constraint is automatically satisfied if we process bottom-up.\n\nBut careful: The required_symbols list contains symbols (like \"root\", \"s1\"). If we remove a node with a required symbol, we can't. But also, after removal, we need to ensure that the required symbols still appear somewhere. That is, the set of symbols of all remaining nodes must include the required symbols. So if a node with a required symbol is removed, that's not allowed. But if a required symbol appears in multiple nodes, we might remove one as long as at least one remains. However, the problem states \"a required symbol\" in the context of \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That implies we cannot remove a node whose symbol is in required_symbols at all? Or we can remove it if the symbol is still present elsewhere? The phrasing \"a required symbol\" likely means a node that has a symbol that is required, and we must preserve at least one occurrence of that symbol. But \"never remove a node with retained children\" is a separate condition. Usually in Perses, you cannot delete a node that has children you want to keep. But here we are deleting entire subtrees.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, I think the safest interpretation from the description \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" This applies to the whole tree after removal. So we can remove a node if after removal (of that node and its descendants) all required symbols are still present in some node. That automatically prevents removal of the last occurrence of a required symbol. But the instruction \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children\" might be redundant: the root is not removable (removable=false), a required symbol is prevented by the condition, and a node with retained children would mean we haven't removed its children yet, but if we remove the node, the children would be removed too, but we might have already decided to keep them? Actually if we process bottom-up, we will remove children first, so when we consider a parent, its children are already gone (removed), so it won't have retained children. So we can ignore that constraint if we process properly.\n\nThus algorithm:\n- Build a mapping from id to node object.\n- Build tree: children lists.\n- Determine initial set of all node ids. We'll simulate removals.\n- Sort candidate nodes (those with removable=True, id != root, symbol not in required_symbols? Actually we should check removable only, but we can include all nodes and then test if removal is allowed.)\n- Sort candidates by depth descending, then by id ascending (tie-breaker).\n- Iterate over candidates in that order:\n    - For each node, check if it is still in the set of nodes (it could have been removed already if its parent was removed? Actually if we remove a parent, all descendants are removed, so we should skip if node is not in set).\n    - Let removed_set = set of nodes that would be removed: this node and all its descendants (recursively).\n    - Check if after removing these nodes, the remaining set still contains at least one node for each required symbol. Also check that root is not removed (root should not be removable anyway).\n    - If allowed, then remove them: add this node's id to removed_node_ids list, and remove all descendants from the node set.\n- After processing all candidates, the remaining nodes are retained. Their ids sorted.\n\nBut careful: The removal order should be the order in which we actually remove nodes. When we remove a parent, we remove many nodes at once. The \"actual removal order\" likely means the order of the nodes we explicitly decided to remove (the root of the removed subtree). The problem says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" The removed_node_ids should be the IDs of nodes that we removed, in the order we removed them (i.e., the order of our decisions). Since we remove a parent and its subtree in one step, we only record the parent's id. That matches typical reduction: you try to delete a node, and if it works, you delete the whole subtree.\n\nNow confirm with examples: The fixtures show cases with nodes and required_symbols [\"root\", \"s1\"]. root is not removable. n1 is not removable, symbol s1 (required). n2 removable, symbol s2 (not required). n3 not removable, s0. n4 removable, s1 (required). n5 not removable, s2. n6 removable, s0. So nodes that are removable: n2, n4, n6. But n4 has symbol s1 which is required, but there is also n1 with s1, so removing n4 might be allowed if n1 retains s1. Let's simulate: depths: n2 depth1, n4 depth2, n6 depth2. Sort by depth desc: n4 and n6 (depth2), then n2 (depth1). Among depth2, tie: id \"n4\" < \"n6\" so n4 first. Check removal of n4: it is removable, not root, its symbol s1 is required but there is another s1 (n1). Removing n4 removes its subtree? n4 has no children (depth2, parent n2, no children listed). So after removal, remaining nodes have s1 from n1. So allowed. Remove n4. Then consider n6: removable, symbol s0 not required, no children. Remove n6? Check required symbols: still root (root) and s1 (n1). So allowed. Remove n6. Then consider n2: depth1, removable. Its children? n4 was already removed, so it has no children (or has children that were removed). So after removal of n2, would required symbols be present? root is root, s1 from n1. So yes. Remove n2. So removed_node_ids = [\"n4\", \"n6\", \"n2\"] in that order. Retained_node_ids = [\"n1\", \"n3\", \"n5\", \"root\"] sorted = [\"n1\",\"n3\",\"n5\",\"root\"]? But note: n5 is not removable, so retained. However, after removal of n2, n4 is gone, but n2's parent is root, so fine. So retained set: root, n1, n3, n5. That seems plausible. The problem expects some specific output? We don't have expected output but we can reason.\n\nNow what about \"utility\"? The problem mentions utility but does not seem to use it in the constraints? It might be unused. The description says \"treat these registered properties as hard contracts rather than soft preferences: grammar_valid, interestingness_preserved, registered_candidate_order, stable_ties.\" But we don't have a concrete interestingness oracle beyond required symbols. So we assume interestingness is that all required symbols are present.\n\nThus implement solve(case) that returns {\"removed_node_ids\": list of strings in removal order, \"retained_node_ids\": sorted list of strings of retained node ids}.\n\nWe'll need to parse the case payload: it has \"nodes\" list and \"required_symbols\" list.\n\nEdge cases: If a removable node has required symbol and it is the only occurrence, must not remove. If a node is not removable, we cannot remove it. The root is not removable.\n\nAlso need to handle the case where removal is infeasible? Probably always feasible since at least root remains and root is required. But if required symbols not present initially? That would be invalid, raise ValueError? The instruction says \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case instead of silently repairing it.\" So if required_symbols list contains symbols not present in any node's symbol, that's infeasible. Also if there is no node with a required symbol? But root is always present with symbol \"root\"? Not necessarily, but in fixtures root has symbol \"root\". However, required_symbols could be arbitrary. So we should check that after minimal set (no removals) all required symbols are present. If not, raise ValueError. Also check that all nodes have necessary fields, etc.\n\nImplementation steps in Python:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes_data = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    # Build node_dict by id\n    node_dict = {}\n    children = {}\n    for n in nodes_data:\n        nid = n[\"id\"]\n        node_dict[nid] = n\n        children[nid] = []\n    for n in nodes_data:\n        pid = n[\"parent\"]\n        if pid is not None:\n            children[pid].append(n[\"id\"])\n    # Check if required symbols are present initially\n    symbols_present = {n[\"symbol\"] for n in node_dict.values()}\n    if not required_symbols.issubset(symbols_present):\n        raise ValueError(\"Required symbols not all present in tree\")\n    # Identify root: parent is None\n    root_id = None\n    for nid, n in node_dict.items():\n        if n[\"parent\"] is None:\n            root_id = nid\n            break\n    if root_id is None:\n        raise ValueError(\"No root node (parent None)\")\n    # Set of currently retained node ids\n    retained = set(node_dict.keys())\n    # List of removed node ids in order\n    removed_order = []\n    # Get all nodes that are removable (and not root)\n    candidates = []\n    for nid, n in node_dict.items():\n        if n[\"removable\"] and nid != root_id:\n            candidates.append(nid)\n    # Sort by depth descending, then id ascending\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (-node_dict[x][\"depth\"], x))\n    # Process\n    for nid in candidates:\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue # already removed as part of parent removal\n        # Compute subtree (including nid)\n        subtree = set()\n        stack = [nid]\n        while stack:\n            cur = stack.pop()\n            if cur in subtree:\n                continue\n            subtree.add(cur)\n            for child in children[cur]:\n                stack.append(child)\n        # Check if removing subtree violates required symbols\n        # Temporarily compute remaining symbols\n        remaining_symbols = set()\n        for other in retained:\n            if other not in subtree:\n                remaining_symbols.add(node_dict[other][\"symbol\"])\n        if required_symbols.issubset(remaining_symbols):\n            # Allowed to remove\n            removed_order.append(nid)\n            retained -= subtree\n    # retained now has nodes not removed\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\n    return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_order, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_ids}\n\nBut careful: The removal of a node should also remove its descendants. In the subtree computation, we include nid and all descendants. That's correct.\n\nNow about the ordering: We are processing candidates sorted, and we remove whole subtrees. If we later process a node that is within an already removed subtree, it will be skipped because not in retained. That's fine.\n\nNow check with the fixture example: Let's simulate manually with code logic. For case 000:\ncandidates: n2 (d1), n4 (d2), n6 (d2). Sorted: n4, n6, n2.\nProcess n4: subtree = {n4}. Retained initially all. Check if removing n4 leaves required symbols: root, n1,s1, n2,s2, n3,s0, n5,s2, n6,s0. required_symbols = {\"root\",\"s1\"}. root present (root), s1 present (n1). So allowed. Remove n4. retained becomes all except n4. removed_order = [\"n4\"].\nNext n6: subtree={n6}. Check remaining symbols: root, n1,s1, n2,s2, n3,s0, n5,s2. required symbols still present. Remove n6. removed_order = [\"n4\",\"n6\"].\nNext n2: subtree = {n2}. But n2's children originally: n4? But n4 already removed, so children list still includes n4? In our children dictionary, children[n2] = [\"n4\"] (since we built from original data). But subtree computation: we start with n2, then add its children recursively. The loop will add n4, even though n4 is already removed from retained. That's fine because subtree is the set of nodes that would be removed if we remove n2, regardless of current retained state. However, we should not remove n4 again, but that's okay because we only remove nodes that are in retained. But the subtree includes n4, which is not in retained, so when we do retained -= subtree, it won't affect because n4 already gone. However, we should be careful: The subtree removal of n2 should also remove any descendants that are still present. Since n4 is already removed, it's not in retained, so safe. But what if n4 had children that are still present? In this case no. So we compute subtree from original structure, which includes n4 even if already removed. That could cause us to attempt to remove n4 again (which is harmless because it's not in retained). However, the condition for removing n2 should only consider that we are removing n2 and all its descendants that are currently in the retained set. But if a descendant was removed earlier, it's fine. The important thing is that after removal, required symbols are present. Removing n2 and its subtree (which currently includes only n2 itself because n4 is gone) still satisfies required symbols. So allowed. Remove n2. removed_order = [\"n4\",\"n6\",\"n2\"]. retained = remaining: root, n1, n3, n5. Sorted: [\"n1\",\"n3\",\"n5\",\"root\"]? Actually root id is \"root\", so sorted order: \"n1\", \"n3\", \"n5\", \"root\". That seems plausible.\n\nFor case 001, similar.\n\nNow one nuance: The description says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" We used ascending string order for tie. That's fine.\n\nAlso \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" We did that.\n\n\"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" Our algorithm prevents removing root (we skip root_id). It prevents removing a node whose symbol is in required_symbols only if there is another occurrence? Actually we don't explicit check symbol, but the condition \"all required symbols remain represented\" covers that. If a node's symbol is required and it's the only occurrence, removal would remove that symbol, so condition fails. So we don't need explicit check for required symbol. For \"node with retained children\": since we process bottom-up, children are removed first, so when we consider a parent, its children are already removed (if they were removable) or not removable. If a child is not removable, then the parent cannot be removed because removing parent would remove that non-removable child, and also the required symbols might be affected. But the algorithm will attempt removal of the parent. If the child is non-removable, then the parent's subtree includes that child. Removing parent would remove that child. The condition would check if required symbols still present. If the non-removable child holds a unique required symbol, removal will fail. So it's fine.\n\nEdge case: What if a node has a child that is not removable but its symbol is not required? Removing the parent would remove that child, but is that allowed? The algorithm would allow it if required symbols still present. However, the problem statement might imply that non-removable nodes are essential and cannot be removed indirectly. But the description says \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" This might mean that if you are considering removing a node, you must ensure that its children are not being retained (i.e., either they are also to be removed, or they are already removed). In our algorithm, we remove the entire subtree, so if a child is non-removable, we would be removing it indirectly, which violates the \"never remove a node that is not removable\" principle. But is that allowed? In Perses, you can delete a non-terminal that has children, but you cannot delete a token? I'm not sure. The problem says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" It doesn't say \"never remove a node that is not removable\". The removable flag indicates whether the node can be considered for removal. So if a node is not removable, we should never remove it directly or indirectly. So we must ensure that we never remove a non-removable node. Therefore, before removing a node, we must check that all nodes in its subtree are removable (or will be removed as part of this operation). But note that non-removable nodes might be required for the grammar. So we should only allow removal of a node if its entire subtree consists of nodes that are either also being removed (which includes the parent) and all are removable? Actually if a non-removable node is in the subtree, we cannot remove it even indirectly. So we should not remove a parent if any of its descendants are not removable. The problem's phrasing \"a node with retained children\" might be interpreted as a node that has children that are still retained (i.e., after the removal, would be retained). But if the children are not removable, they must be retained, so the parent cannot be removed. So we should add a check: the subtree we plan to remove must contain only removable nodes (including the parent). But what about nodes that are removable but we haven't removed them yet? That's fine, they can be removed. So condition: every node in subtree must have removable=True (or be the root? but root is not in subtree because we skip root). This aligns with the idea that you can only delete a grammar rule that is entirely optional? Hmm.\n\nLooking at fixture: node n3 is not removable (depth1). If we try to remove its parent (root) that's not allowed. But what if we try to remove n2's child n4 (removable) that's fine. n5 is not removable, depth2 under n3. Can we remove n3? n3 is not removable, so no. Can we remove n3's parent root? root not removable. So n5 is never removed. That's consistent. In our algorithm for case 000, we didn't attempt to remove n3 because it's not removable. But consider if there was a parent that is removable and has a non-removable child. Our algorithm would try to remove the parent, and the subtree includes the non-removable child. That would indirectly remove the non-removable child, which should not be allowed. So we need to prevent that. Therefore modify: before removal, check that all nodes in subtree have removable=True (or are already removed? but they are in retained, so they are not removed). Since we are removing them, we require them to be removable. So condition: for each node in subtree, node_dict[node][\"removable\"] must be True, except maybe if we allow the parent itself (which we already know is removable). But if a descendant is not removable, we cannot remove it. So we should skip removal of the parent if any descendant is not removable.\n\nThus update in the loop:\n\n# Compute subtree\nsubtree = set()\nstack = [nid]\nwhile stack:\n    cur = stack.pop()\n    if cur in subtree:\n        continue\n    subtree.add(cur)\n    for child in children[cur]:\n        stack.append(child)\n\n# Check if any node in subtree is not removable (except maybe nid itself which is removable)\n# But also check that all nodes in subtree are currently in retained? They should be, because we haven't removed them.\nallow = True\nfor node_in_sub in subtree:\n    if not node_dict[node_in_sub][\"removable\"]:\n        allow = False\n        break\nif not allow:\n    continue\n\nThen also check required symbols condition.\n\nThis ensures we never indirectly remove non-removable nodes.\n\nNow check with fixture: n2's subtree originally includes n4 (removable) and maybe n4's children (none). So all removable, fine. n2 itself removable. n4's subtree includes only n4, removable. n6's subtree includes only n6, removable. So all pass.\n\nNow consider a hypothetical: if n3 was removable but n5 was not, then removing n3 would involve n5, which is not removable, so skip. That seems correct.\n\nNow we produce the code. Also need to handle missing fields or invalid data: raise ValueError.\n\nNow the output must be exactly JSON with keys \"implementation\". The implementation value is the Python source code as a string, properly escaped. We'll need to write the code as a string.\n\nAlso note: The problem says \"Do not rename fields or change their nesting.\" The output schema only requires \"removed_node_ids\" and \"retained_node_ids\". So we just return that.\n\nNow produce final answer: a JSON object with \"implementation\" containing the Python source.\n\nWe need to ensure code is valid Python. Use proper indentation. Escape special characters in the JSON string.\n\nLet's write the code with a function solve(case) that uses only standard library (json for output? Actually not needed because we return a dict which the evaluator will JSON-serialize. But we can just return a dict). So no imports needed.\n\nImplementation:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes_data = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    node_dict = {}\n    children = {}\n    for n in nodes_data:\n        nid = n[\"id\"]\n        node_dict[nid] = n\n        children[nid] = []\n    for n in nodes_data:\n        pid = n[\"parent\"]\n        if pid is not None:\n            children[pid].append(n[\"id\"])\n    # Check required symbols present\n    all_symbols = {n[\"symbol\"] for n in node_dict.values()}\n    if not required_symbols.issubset(all_symbols):\n        raise ValueError(\"Required symbols not all present\")\n    # Find root\n    root_id = None\n    for nid, n in node_dict.items():\n        if n[\"parent\"] is None:\n            root_id = nid\n            break\n    if root_id is None:\n        raise ValueError(\"No root node\")\n    # Initialize retained set\n    retained = set(node_dict.keys())\n    # Candidates: removable and not root\n    candidates = [nid for nid, n in node_dict.items() if n[\"removable\"] and nid != root_id]\n    # Sort by depth descending, then id ascending\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (-node_dict[x][\"depth\"], x))\n    removed_order = []\n    for nid in candidates:\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        # Compute subtree\n        subtree = set()\n        stack = [nid]\n        while stack:\n            cur = stack.pop()\n            if cur in subtree:\n                continue\n            subtree.add(cur)\n            for child in children[cur]:\n                stack.append(child)\n        # Check that all nodes in subtree are removable (since we will remove them)\n        all_removable = all(node_dict[n][\"removable\"] for n in subtree)\n        if not all_removable:\n            continue\n        # Check required symbols after removal\n        remaining_symbols = set()\n        for n_ret in retained:\n            if n_ret not in subtree:\n                remaining_symbols.add(node_dict[n_ret][\"symbol\"])\n        if required_symbols.issubset(remaining_symbols):\n            removed_order.append(nid)\n            retained -= subtree\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\n    return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_order, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_ids}\n\nWe should also consider that \"removable\" property might be missing? But fixtures have it. If missing, raise ValueError.\n\nNow produce JSON envelope. The code should be a single string.\n\nWe'll write the JSON:\n\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes_data = case[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n    required_symbols = set(case[\\\"required_symbols\\\"])\\n    node_dict = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    for n in nodes_data:\\n        nid = n[\\\"id\\\"]\\n        node_dict[nid] = n\\n        children[nid] = []\\n    for n in nodes_data:\\n        pid = n[\\\"parent\\\"]\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children[pid].append(n[\\\"id\\\"])\\n    all_symbols = {n[\\\"symbol\\\"] for n in node_dict.values()}\\n    if not required_symbols.issubset(all_symbols):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Required symbols not all present\\\")\\n    root_id = None\\n    for nid, n in node_dict.items():\\n        if n[\\\"parent\\\"] is None:\\n            root_id = nid\\n            break\\n    if root_id is None:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"No root node\\\")\\n    retained = set(node_dict.keys())\\n    candidates = [nid for nid, n in node_dict.items() if n[\\\"removable\\\"] and nid != root_id]\\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (-node_dict[x][\\\"depth\\\"], x))\\n    removed_order = []\\n    for nid in candidates:\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        subtree = set()\\n        stack = [nid]\\n        while stack:\\n            cur = stack.pop()\\n            if cur in subtree:\\n                continue\\n            subtree.add(cur)\\n            for child in children[cur]:\\n                stack.append(child)\\n        all_removable = all(node_dict[n][\\\"removable\\\"] for n in subtree)\\n        if not all_removable:\\n            continue\\n        remaining_symbols = set()\\n        for n_ret in retained:\\n            if n_ret not in subtree:\\n                remaining_symbols.add(node_dict[n_ret][\\\"symbol\\\"])\\n        if required_symbols.issubset(remaining_symbols):\\n            removed_order.append(nid)\\n            retained -= subtree\\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed_order, \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_ids}\"\n}\n\nMake sure to escape quotes and backslashes properly. In the above, we have double quotes inside the string, they need to be escaped with backslash. Also newlines as \\n. The entire string should be on one line in the JSON.\n\nWe can write it compactly.\n\nLet's produce the final output."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2169,"completion_tokens":7115,"total_tokens":9284,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":6584},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":121},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_pro